    content: `WARM FRONT and COLD FRONT clouds differ because the two boundaries have very different shapes and lift the warm air at very different rates. A WARM FRONT is where advancing warm air replaces retreating cold air. Its frontal surface is SHALLOW and gently sloping (roughly 1:100 to 1:300), so the warm air rises SLOWLY as it glides up and over the cold-air wedge. This produces a broad, orderly SEQUENCE of layered STRATIFORM clouds spreading far (600–1000 km) ahead of the surface front, so you can see the weather coming a day in advance: first high, wispy CIRRUS; then CIRROSTRATUS (a milky veil, often with a 22° halo around the sun or moon); then lowering, thickening ALTOSTRATUS (sun looks like a dim disc "through ground glass"); then low, dark NIMBOSTRATUS with STEADY, light-to-moderate RAIN or SNOW over a wide area for many hours. Fog and drizzle are common in the cool air ahead. After the front passes, skies partly clear and temperature and humidity rise. A COLD FRONT is where advancing cold air displaces warm air. Its leading edge is STEEP and blunt (about 1:50 to 1:150 — the surface friction makes it bulge forward at the bottom), so the dense cold air aggressively UNDERCUTS and forces the warm air upward FAST. That rapid lift produces deep CONVECTIVE clouds — CUMULUS congestus building into towering CUMULONIMBUS — concentrated in a NARROW band along or just ahead of the front (sometimes as a squall line). The weather is a brief but intense burst: heavy showers or thunderstorms, possibly hail and gusty/shifting winds, a sharp pressure fall then rise, and a marked temperature and dew-point drop. Clearing behind is rapid, often to bright skies with fair-weather cumulus. SUMMARY: warm front = widespread, layered clouds and prolonged steady precipitation; cold front = a narrow line of tall clouds and short, heavy, often thundery precipitation.`,
