    content: `A HEAT ADVISORY and an EXCESSIVE HEAT WARNING are both US National Weather Service heat products, and BOTH mean DANGEROUSLY HOT weather is expected — neither has ANYTHING to do with cold or freezing temperatures. They differ only in SEVERITY, following the standard NWS "watch / warning / advisory" system: (1) an EXCESSIVE HEAT WATCH means dangerous heat is POSSIBLE in the next 24–72 hours (conditions are favourable but not certain) — a heads-up to prepare. (2) A HEAT ADVISORY is the LOWER alert level, issued when dangerous heat is IMMINENT or occurring but at the less extreme end: typically a HEAT INDEX (the "feels-like" temperature combining air temperature and humidity) forecast to reach roughly 100–105 °F (38–41 °C) for at least two hours over two or more days, or a shorter spell slightly higher. At this level, heat cramps and heat exhaustion are possible, mainly for the elderly, the very young, the ill, outdoor workers, and anyone over-exerting. (3) An EXCESSIVE HEAT WARNING is the HIGHER, more urgent level, issued when the heat index is forecast to reach about 105–110 °F (41–43 °C) or higher — and critically, when overnight lows stay very warm (e.g. above ~75 °F / 24 °C), so the body gets no chance to cool down and recover. At this level the heat is a threat to ANYONE, not just vulnerable groups, and heat stroke and heat-related death become likely without air conditioning and precautions. IMPORTANT: the exact trigger temperatures VARY BY NWS region, because populations acclimatise to their local climate — a heat index that triggers a warning in Seattle or Boston might only rate an advisory in Phoenix or Dallas. In 2024 the NWS also introduced an experimental "HeatRisk" colour scale to complement these text products.`,
