    category: 'Meteorology',
    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between the wet bulb temperature and the dry bulb temperature ordinary air temperature versus thermometer wrapped in wet wick evaporative cooling humidity psychrometer wet bulb 35 survivability limit',
      'the DRY BULB temperature is just the ORDINARY AIR TEMPERATURE measured by a normal thermometer shaded from sun and rain and shielded from moisture it is what people mean by the temperature',
      'the WET BULB temperature is measured by a thermometer whose bulb is wrapped in a wet cloth wick with air moving over it water evaporates from the wick and cools the bulb the drier the air the more evaporation and the more cooling so the bigger the gap between wet bulb and dry bulb when the air is saturated 100 percent relative humidity no evaporation happens and wet bulb equals dry bulb',
      'the wet bulb temperature is the lowest temperature you can reach by evaporative cooling so it sets the limit for sweat cooling swamp coolers and cooling towers a sustained wet bulb near 35 C 95 F is considered the theoretical survivability limit for humans because sweat can no longer shed body heat the pair of readings a psychrometer gives is used to derive relative humidity and dew point',
    ],
    content: `The DRY-BULB temperature is simply the ORDINARY AIR TEMPERATURE — what a normal thermometer reads when it is shaded from direct sun, sheltered from precipitation, and kept dry and well-ventilated (as in a Stevenson screen). It is what everyone means by "the temperature." The WET-BULB temperature is what a thermometer reads when its bulb is wrapped in a wet cloth "wick" (soaked in distilled water) with air flowing across it. Water EVAPORATES from the wick, and because evaporation absorbs latent heat, it COOLS the bulb below the actual air temperature. How much cooling depends on how dry the air is: in DRY air, lots of water evaporates, so the wet-bulb reading drops far below the dry-bulb reading (a large "wet-bulb depression"); in HUMID air, little evaporates, so the two readings are close; and when the air is fully SATURATED (100% relative humidity), no net evaporation occurs and the wet-bulb temperature EQUALS the dry-bulb temperature. An instrument holding both thermometers (often a "sling psychrometer," whirled through the air) uses the two readings and psychrometric tables to derive RELATIVE HUMIDITY and DEW POINT. WHY THE WET-BULB MATTERS: it is the LOWEST temperature achievable by evaporative cooling, so it governs how well sweating, "swamp" coolers, and power-plant cooling towers can shed heat. The human body cools mainly by evaporating sweat, and it cannot cool below the wet-bulb temperature of the surrounding air. A sustained wet-bulb temperature near 35 °C (95 °F) is regarded as the theoretical limit of human survivability — at that point a healthy person at rest in the shade with unlimited water can no longer dump metabolic heat and will overheat within hours. This is distinct from the "wet-bulb globe temperature" (WBGT), a weighted heat-stress index used by the military and athletics that also factors in sunshine and wind.`,
