      'what is the difference between the tropopause and the stratopause atmospheric layer boundaries troposphere stratosphere mesosphere temperature reversal altitude 10 km versus 50 km',
      'the atmosphere has layers defined by how temperature changes with height from the ground up TROPOSPHERE STRATOSPHERE MESOSPHERE THERMOSPHERE and the pauses are the BOUNDARIES between them where the temperature trend reverses',
      'the TROPOPAUSE is the boundary between the TROPOSPHERE below and the STRATOSPHERE above at roughly 8 to 18 km high about 17 km at the equator 9 km at the poles it is where temperature STOPS falling with height it marks the top of weather almost all clouds storms and water vapour are below it and it acts as a lid that thunderstorm anvils spread out against',
      'the STRATOPAUSE is much higher the boundary between the STRATOSPHERE below and the MESOSPHERE above at roughly 50 to 55 km it is where temperature STOPS RISING with height the stratosphere warms upward because ozone absorbs ultraviolet sunlight so the stratopause is a local temperature MAXIMUM around 0 C above it in the mesosphere temperature falls again to the coldest point in the whole atmosphere at the mesopause',
