    content: `The TROPOPAUSE and the STRATOPAUSE are both "pauses" — BOUNDARIES between atmospheric layers, each marking a point where the vertical temperature trend REVERSES — but they are at completely different altitudes and separate different layers. Earth's atmosphere is divided by its temperature profile into (from the ground up) the TROPOSPHERE, STRATOSPHERE, MESOSPHERE, and THERMOSPHERE. The TROPOPAUSE is the boundary between the TROPOSPHERE (below) and the STRATOSPHERE (above), at roughly 8–18 km altitude — about 17–18 km over the equator, dropping to 8–9 km over the poles, and lower still in winter and behind cold fronts. In the troposphere, temperature FALLS with height (about 6.5 °C/km on average); at the tropopause that decline STOPS (an "isothermal" layer), and above it, in the lower stratosphere, temperature is steady or rises. The tropopause is effectively "the top of the weather": nearly all clouds, precipitation, storms, and water vapour are confined below it, and it acts as a stable "lid" — which is why vigorous thunderstorm updrafts flatten out into anvil clouds when they hit it (and only the strongest "overshooting tops" briefly punch through). Jet streams flow along breaks in the tropopause. The STRATOPAUSE is MUCH higher — the boundary between the STRATOSPHERE (below) and the MESOSPHERE (above), at roughly 50–55 km altitude. Through the stratosphere, temperature RISES with height, because OZONE there absorbs ultraviolet solar radiation and heats the air. That warming reaches its peak at the stratopause, making it a local temperature MAXIMUM of around 0 °C (−15 to +5 °C). Above the stratopause, in the mesosphere, temperature FALLS again with height, bottoming out near −90 °C at the MESOPAUSE (~85 km) — the coldest place in Earth's atmosphere. So: tropopause ≈ 10 km, top of the troposphere, temperature stops falling; stratopause ≈ 50 km, top of the stratosphere, temperature stops rising.`,
