    content: `A COLD FRONT PASSAGE and a DRY LINE PASSAGE both involve an air-mass boundary sweeping past, but they separate air masses that differ in different properties. A COLD FRONT is a TEMPERATURE boundary — the leading edge of a cooler (and usually drier) air mass advancing to displace warmer air. As it passes a location you observe: a WIND SHIFT (typically veering to the west or northwest in the Northern Hemisphere), a distinct DROP IN TEMPERATURE (and dew point), a pressure fall ahead followed by a sharp rise behind, and frequently a narrow band of showers or thunderstorms along the front where the dense cold air wedges under and lifts the warm air. Skies usually clear behind it. A DRY LINE (also called a "dew-point front" or "dew line") is a MOISTURE boundary with LITTLE OR NO temperature contrast across it. It separates warm, HUMID air (in the US, maritime tropical air from the Gulf of Mexico) on its EAST side from hot, DRY air (continental tropical air from the desert Southwest and the Mexican plateau) on its WEST side. As a dry line passes eastward, the DEW POINT plunges dramatically — often 15–30 °F (8–17 °C) in an hour or less — relative humidity crashes, the sky may go from hazy/cumulus-dotted to deep blue, and visibility improves; but the actual air TEMPERATURE often stays the same or even RISES (dry air heats up faster). The dry line is one of the most important SEVERE-WEATHER and TORNADO triggers on the US Southern Plains (west Texas, Oklahoma, Kansas) in spring. It typically BULGES eastward during the heat of the afternoon (mixing brings dry air down and forward) and RETREATS westward overnight. Supercell thunderstorms often "fire" right along the dry line in the late afternoon, where the moist air is forced up over the nose of the advancing dry air (or where a "triple point" — the intersection of the dry line and a cold front — provides extra lift).`,
